e :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"text:format\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"5b2f61fd-114b-3c42-8a91-f6f324721187\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"event:point\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"e3248d03-3193-3326-82dc-10bba4028c17\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"behavior:latest-value\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"29084ef0-2c99-33e4-ab25-1a59bb6954b5\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"behavior:event-filter\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"bbd55eda-8267-36aa-a11b-b4cd86cd0328\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"behavior:map\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"7a2b7773-5ba7-36ac-a138-cbe7e4f83bd0\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"behavior:scan\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"c2fc2f06-ef58-3f5d-b0a2-81bb3db4ce13\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"behavior:source\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"24939bc6-4e5e-36b6-858a-1b7af5da2db3\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"message-to-user\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"2d6c61cc-3812-3f71-91ce-e40be1fc49cb\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"model:request\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"78f2c4f4-af10-3841-8733-0e81a78c920c\"}, :state :known, :headline \"callable; see language-introduction-cell\"} {:title \"user-ingress\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"ea01d4a4-d510-3558-ab61-38f57d32a81d\"}, :state :known, :headline \"unknown\"} {:title \"memory-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"214cf34e-9728-38aa-866e-20afe43453e8\"}, :state :known, :headline \"{:user #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"4c86cee9-917e-300b-b50b-a83d4d2cc5e9\\\"}, :model-output #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"dc373e3b-bf2e-315d-875c-331bc51f942f\\\"}, :deliveries #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"dec4e1d4-538f-3b7a-bb63-91f915881bc7\\\"}, :diagnostics #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"dceaf5a1-66a0-3761-95de-9f81c91c5148\\\"}}\"} {:title \"experiment-diagnostic-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"4d423f62-043b-3a90-a232-d66f1d8722e7\"}, :state :known, :headline \":not-started\"} {:title \"observe-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"6a9ff40c-5fd1-3d84-87d3-19c1026fca37\"}, :state :unknown, :headline \"unknown\"} {:title \"language-introduction-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"aec38016-528f-3e1e-89f0-ede169fbbfe5\"}, :state :known, :headline \"Lain is a connected dataflow language, not Clojure. Return raw parenthesized forms only; triple-backtick fences are invalid source. A callable is invoked as (f x); (-> x f) writes to the callable cell and does NOT call f. Only binding/argument vectors use literal brackets; construct data vectors with seq:empty and seq:append. Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects. A scan takes its source as its first argument; do not write events to the scan result. behavior:scan, map, and event-filter produce behavior objects, not ordinary records or vectors. Use behavior:latest-value to project a selected behavior value before passing to ordinary primitives. An optional fallback makes an empty behavior usable without inventing an occurrence. seq:map projects one key, so (seq:map :role xs) returns role strings, not message records. Model messages must be records with string :role and :content. System instructions must be first. The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable. You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source. (define name expression) binds a cell. (define pending) declares an unknown cell. (network [x y] expression) defines a callable. (let [x expression y expression] body) binds locals. (-> value target-cell) connects cells. (if condition yes no) selects a branch. Qualified names avoid editor collisions. Use data:record for immutable plain records, NOT struct, when passing data to primitives. (data:record :key value) constructs a record; (data:get :key record) reads it. seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element; seq:concat [vector ...] concatenates vectors into one flat vector; for example (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) 1) (seq:append (seq:empty) 2)) returns a vector containing 1 and 2; seq:last [vector] reads the last element. seq:map [key vector] projects a key over a vector. data:equal? [a b] compares values. event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence. Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences; connect its output to a behavior:source or scan. text:format [template & values] formats %s placeholders; for example (text:format \\\"item %s\\\" 7). Example individual operations: (define values (behavior:source)); (-> (event:point \\\"sample\\\" 1 7) values); (define sums (behavior:scan values + 0)). behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->. behavior:scan [source reducer initial] accumulates ordered occurrences using a pure two-argument Lain callable; maps, filters, and reducers construct values and must not perform IO. behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] selects occurrences with a one-argument callable. behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values. behavior:latest-value [behavior optional-fallback] projects its most recent value to an ordinary supported cell; before the first occurrence it returns the optional fallback, or remains unknown if no fallback was supplied. For example (behavior:latest-value values 0). Repeated identical events are suppressed; sources and scans are independent. model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records } to the existing model. Roles are strings system/user/assistant; content is a string. user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content. Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id. message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content and returns that record with :acknowledged true. Its output, not generated source, is the delivered message. source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially, preserving earlier forms if a later one fails. environment:overview [environment] lists visible binding titles, headlines and references, including unknowns. cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference without waiting for its value. inspect:cell [reference] exposes :content, :strongest, :input-connections (producing connections), and :output-connections (consuming connections). Native semantic tracers are available; distinguish cell producers/consumers from callable argument/result ports.\"} {:title \"construction-review-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"5a398331-646b-3f49-9a25-3b76585961cb\"}, :state :known, :headline \"A previous generated setup compiled but did not connect requests or history. Diagnostic review: each call to behavior:source creates a DISTINCT source, so writing to another unnamed instance does not feed a previously created scan. Event identity and time must vary with actual events; constants user/1 and assistant/2 cannot identify three turns. A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source. A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation; model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback. seq:map with key :role returns strings and therefore is unsuitable as the request messages vector. No generated definitions from previous attempts exist in this fresh child.\"} {:title \"context-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"302c51ff-677a-372f-a1ba-95c01e259135\"}, :state :known, :headline \"Construct a small live conversation network in Lain. Keep reasoning brief. Return only executable Lain forms, without markdown. Discover the environment bindings below. Your setup must build history with the supplied tested temporal reducers, preserving chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies. Connect user-ingress to model requests and define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages. Provider thinking and generated source are never conversation replies. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling your reply function with a new assistant message. Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention you have defined, so future requests know to produce executable source. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history. Use the same model throughout. Do not send a request until a user event arrives. Unknown inputs suspend a computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request. model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails.\"}]"}]